hexagon: htp: softplus in the oracle form
Fold-into: patches/hexagon-kernels/0004-hexagon-htp-softplus-on-the-vector-unit.patch

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The f32 path of the HVX softplus computes relu(x) + log1p(e^-|x|) with an
accurate log1p. The CPU reference (and the oracle) computes
logf(1.0f + expf(x)) for x <= 20, which rounds 1 + e^x to an f32 first. For a
negative x this rounding moves the result by up to 2^-24 absolute, thus the
two forms differ by about 256 ulp near x = -5 and by up to 16389 ulp near
x = -9. Against the oracle form with correctly rounded expf and logf, the v79
simulator gives for x >= -10 a mean of 296 ulp, and 73.8 % of the values are
within 4 ulp. The app graphs reach this op: each gated delta net layer
computes the softplus of its gate (16 values for each token in the 2B, 32 in
the 4B).

This patch rounds 1 + e^x to an f32 for a negative x, as the reference does:
t = (1 + t) - 1, with both steps in f32. The second step is exact, thus
log1p(t) gives log of the rounded sum. The simulator then gives a mean of
0.37 ulp, and 99.7 % of the values are within 4 ulp. Where the exp of the
backend differs by one ulp from expf, the rounded sum can move by one ulp of
1, thus the largest difference stays 2^-23 absolute. The cycles of one call do
not change (716 for 16 values, 706 for 32, 702 for 128).

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-softplus.h b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-softplus.h
index 49a1ca8..792a20e 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-softplus.h
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-softplus.h
@@ -3,16 +3,23 @@
 //
 // softplus(x) = log(1 + e^x) = relu(x) + log(1 + e^-|x|). The identity holds for every x and it
 // has no cancellation: relu(x) is exact in the float domain and the second term is a smooth bump
-// in (0, log 2]. The op of the checkout evaluates logf(1.0f + expf(x)) one element at a time.
+// in (0, log 2]. The CPU reference (the oracle form) evaluates logf(1.0f + expf(x)) for x <= 20.
 //
 // Two paths, both of which write the same result the CPU reference writes for x > 20 (the result
 // is x, because the bump is below the resolution of an f32 there).
 //
-//   hvx_softplus_f32_aa    t = e^-|x| with the exp of this backend, then log1p(t) = t * P(t) with
-//                          P of degree 9 on [0, 1]. P(0) = 1 and the factor t is exact, thus
-//                          this path keeps its relative accuracy where the result is e^x and no
-//                          fixed-point number can hold it. Measured against a float64 reference:
-//                          NMSE 7.1e-18, worst relative error 2.3e-7 over x in [-30, 30].
+//   hvx_softplus_f32_aa    the oracle form to a few ulp. t = e^-|x| with the exp of this backend.
+//                          For a negative x (t = e^x), t becomes (1 + t) - 1 with both steps in
+//                          f32, as the reference rounds 1 + e^x before its logf, and the second
+//                          step is exact. Then log1p(t) = t * P(t) with P of degree 9 on
+//                          [0, 1], thus the result is log of the rounded sum. The rounded sum has
+//                          an absolute error of up to 2^-24, thus against the exact function the
+//                          relative error grows for a large negative x (the result is 0 below
+//                          -16.6), the same as the reference. The model of this path in the
+//                          generator (--check) gives a mean of 0.45 ulp against the oracle
+//                          form for x >= -10, and 99.5 % of the values within 4 ulp. Where the
+//                          exp differs by one ulp from expf, the rounded sum can move by one
+//                          ulp of 1, thus the largest difference is 2^-23 absolute.
 //
 //   hvx_softplus_i16_f32_aa  D(u) = log(2) - log(1 + e^-u) from a piecewise int16 table, then
 //                          softplus = relu(x) + log(2) - D(|x|). One polynomial of degree 4
@@ -102,6 +109,15 @@ static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) void hvx_softplus_f32_x4(const HVX_
         t[i] = hvx_vec_exp_f32(t[i]);
     }
 
+    // For a negative x, the rounded sum of the reference: t = (1 + t) - 1, both steps in f32. A
+    // negative f32 is a negative int32, thus the word compare with 0 selects the negative lanes.
+    const HVX_Vector one = Q6_V_vsplat_R(0x3f800000);
+    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
+        const HVX_Vector s  = Q6_Vsf_equals_Vqf32(Q6_Vqf32_vadd_VsfVsf(one, t[i]));
+        const HVX_Vector ts = Q6_Vsf_equals_Vqf32(Q6_Vqf32_vsub_VsfVsf(s, one));
+        t[i] = Q6_V_vmux_QVV(Q6_Q_vcmp_gt_VwVw(zero, x[i]), ts, t[i]);
+    }
+
     // log1p(t) = t * P(t), by Horner over the four chains. The first step multiplies the top
     // coefficient as the IEEE single that it is, thus acc holds a qf32 number from its first
     // value and each Q6_Vsf_equals_Vqf32 below gets an operand of the correct type.
